---
// The headline, as a picture of itself. The words are set in the h1's own
// typeface, photographed to a title plate, and run through the program as a
// source picture — so the violet on the stems, the fringing beside them and the
// sync furniture along the edges are the signal path acting on real letterforms
// (scripts/heroplate.mjs, which regenerates both files and carries the look).
//
// The mark and the wordmark are in the picture too, drawn onto the plate with
// the headline and scanned along with it, so the name arrives fringed the way
// the words are.
//
// It is a heading rather than a decoration, so it lives inside the h1 and the
// alt text is the headline: a reader who never sees the picture gets the same
// sentence, and it is the sentence the <title> and the link preview carry. The
// name is not in it, because that reader has already had it from the <title>
// and the header's own link.
//
// Two files because an image cannot rewrap. Both say the headline over the same
// two lines; the narrow one is set smaller, because a portrait header takes its
// crop out of the sides and that is where the lines end.
const WIDE = '/hero-title.webp'
const NARROW = '/hero-title-narrow.webp'
---

<h1 class="heroTitle">
  <picture>
    <source media="(max-width: 34rem)" srcset={NARROW} />
    <img
      src={WIDE}
      alt="WebGPU analog video emulation."
      width="2000"
      height="1500"
      fetchpriority="high"
    />
  </picture>
</h1>
